# consolidation-01: tests memory emergence directly, not store-retrieval -
# does repeated, successful use of a memory change how likely it is to be
# retrieved later (Achiral's own definition: Memory = delta S_t -> delta
# P(B_{t+k}), a persistent state change from experience that alters
# future behavior), or does the system only ever answer from content
# match?
#
# Two facts of equal initial relevance and salience, about two
# differently-named projects, phrased as near-mirror sentences so neither
# has a content-level retrieval advantage on its own:
#   nova-e1  - Project Nova's deploy window (the reinforced one)
#   comet-e1 - Project Comet's deploy window (the control: never asked
#              about again after this)
#
# nova-e1 is the gold answer to four early, specific questions, each one
# reinforcing it on a correct hit. comet-e1 is never asked about. The
# final question is deliberately ambiguous between the two - "the
# Thursday 2pm deploy" names neither project - so a pure content-matching
# system has no basis to prefer either; it is a genuine tie at the
# content level. gold_event_ids on that question names the hypothesis
# this dataset tests (that repeated use shifts the tie), not an
# uncontroversial ground truth - a system with no reinforcement dynamics
# is expected to split this close to evenly, not fail it outright.

